mission 1 "003-object-types" "1" "Mission 003: give object types their own C++ boundary"
requires "001_stable_object_identity"
requires "002_debugging_invariants"
requires "u1-boundaries"
scope "read" "src/main.cpp"
scope "edit" "src/UI/AppUISetup.cpp"
scope "edit" "src/Objects/ObjectTypes.h"
scope "edit" "src/Objects/ObjectTypes.cpp"
scope "edit" "visualstudio/Pandata.bank.vcxproj"
step "brief" "Explain" "ack" "One useful extraction, not a rewrite"
why "PandaBank's object-type list, ObjectType enum and ObjectTypeName function currently live near the top of AppUISetup.cpp. Their job is naming kinds of objects, not drawing widgets. Moving that existing responsibility into a header and implementation gives the next code a small reusable boundary. Keep your X-macro approach and all current behavior. Leave BaseObjects, drawing, spawning, stable IDs and debug assertions alone. You will make the edits in Visual Studio; University will explain the steps and save your observations, but will not write the refactor for you. Allow roughly 45-90 minutes; quit whenever you need a break."
action "Use git-status to inspect your work and files/view to inspect the existing source. Preserve unrelated edits. If you want a checkpoint, make it explicitly in your usual Git tool. Type next when ready."
expect "Only the ObjectType/name responsibility is in scope. No PandaBank file has been changed by this lesson."
more "The two new learner-owned files are src/Objects/ObjectTypes.h and src/Objects/ObjectTypes.cpp. Add them through Visual Studio when you reach the attempt. The project may also update its optional .filters grouping file; that is IDE bookkeeping, not code. No additional library is needed."
professional "A renderer-independent naming boundary is a useful first split. Extracting BaseObjects now would mix this compile/link lesson with renderer and ownership changes."
end
step "predict" "Predict" "response" "Predict the compile and link jobs"
why "A caller needs to know the type and function declaration during compilation. The linker later needs one compiled definition of the non-inline function. An include makes declarations visible but does not add a .cpp to the Visual Studio build. Your existing code works because its caller and definition share one translation unit. Splitting them makes those two jobs visible. Predict before editing so a failure tells you which connection to inspect rather than sending you into random project settings."
action "Type answer followed by one sentence: what belongs in the shared header, and what failure would you expect if the new implementation is not compiled? Then next."
expect "A prediction is saved as learner-reported evidence, not automatically graded."
hint "Separate knowing a function's signature from supplying its compiled body."
syntax "declaration" "Describes a name and type to a caller; a function declaration normally ends with a semicolon."
syntax "definition" "Supplies the function body; a non-inline external function normally needs one definition in the program."
end
step "trace" "Inspect" "response" "Find every use before moving anything"
why "The same X-macro list also drives drawing dispatch later in AppUISetup.cpp. Search for OBJECT_TYPE_LIST, ObjectType and ObjectTypeName before choosing what the header exposes. Keeping the existing list as the single source avoids accidentally diverging the enum and displayed names. Do not replace the approach merely to match someone else's preferred style. First establish what the remaining UI implementation still needs to see. This is a dependency inspection, not a request to redesign object storage."
action "Use view 3 or Visual Studio Find All References/search. Type answer with one use outside the top naming block that must still compile after the move. Then next."
expect "You can identify the naming block and at least one remaining consumer."
hint "Look in BaseObjects::Draw and the spawning/name assignment path."
professional "Macros are text substitution, so scope the temporary X definition carefully at each expansion. Keeping a small deliberate list is different from letting unrelated macros leak everywhere."
end
step "attempt" "ShortAnswer" "response" "Create the boundary yourself"
why "Create the focused header and .cpp in the real PandaBank project. Move the existing naming responsibility without changing its behavior. Decide which declarations and shared list the header must expose, which includes each file actually needs, and where the one function body belongs. Make AppUISetup.cpp use that header and ensure Visual Studio compiles the new .cpp. Do not include a .cpp file to bypass the linker. Nothing in University creates these files or supplies an answer patch."
action "In Visual Studio, make the extraction in the scoped files. Keep the type order, names and fallback behavior unchanged. Type answer with the interface you chose and one reason for it; then next."
expect "The naming responsibility has one implementation and its consumers still have the declarations they need."
hint "Start with the enum, the list it expands, and the existing function signature. Leave its callers unchanged initially."
hint "A header guard or pragma once prevents repeated header declarations in one translation unit."
hint "If the UI still expands OBJECT_TYPE_LIST, the list must remain visible to that translation unit."
more "A source file listed only as a header/None item is not compiled as C++. Check the project item type if the definition is missing at link time. Do not edit thirdparty or replace the renderer."
end
step "verify" "Review" "response" "Build, run and diagnose the real product"
why "University's run-check exercises the University engine, not this PandaBank change. Use the existing Visual Studio PandaBank project in Debug/x64 and Release/x64. Run the app and create more than one object: names, separate widget identity and the existing Debug assertions must still behave as before. A missing declaration is a compile-stage clue; an unresolved external is a link-stage clue; duplicate definitions usually indicate the body was copied instead of owned once. Preserve the first useful error rather than changing unrelated settings."
action "Build and run PandaBank in Debug and Release. Type answer with the results, one observed object behavior and any compiler/linker diagnosis (or none). Then next."
expect "Actual build/run observations are recorded. A saved answer alone does not claim that those builds passed."
hint "Use feedback for a short compiler-versus-linker checklist. Open Visual Studio with open-vs if needed."
end
step "transfer" "Review" "response" "Explain the boundary and inspect your diff"
why "A working build is useful evidence, but understanding the boundary lets you repeat it on the next responsibility. Inspect your Git diff for accidental edits to drawing, IDs, assertions or other unrelated work. Explain how another .cpp would learn the ObjectTypeName declaration and how the program obtains its body. You do not need to add another type, alter the renderer or create a second demonstration project. This small transfer explanation keeps the lesson focused and gives a reviewer something more useful than a screenshot of a green build."
action "Use git-diff or your Git tool. Type answer explaining the declaration-to-definition path and one scope check you made; then next."
expect "The review can distinguish a deliberate extraction from a copied solution."
professional "Alternative layouts are acceptable when they preserve one coherent responsibility, correct compile/link behavior and the accepted X-macro route. Review behavior and tradeoffs, not an exact expected diff."
end
step "checkpoint" "Checkpoint" "ack" "Record the attempt for review"
why "Your answers, hint use and position are saved locally. Recording the final step marks the prepared evidence as collected; it does not certify the refactor, promote concept confidence or make a Git commit. Use report to display the evidence for review alongside your source diff and build results. A reviewer can accept the implementation, identify a tradeoff or request a small correction without making you repeat completed orientation steps. The next curricular topic after this accepted boundary is portable build reasoning. It is not unlocked by an AI claim."
action "Type next to record the checkpoint, then report to copy your saved observations. Commit only when you intentionally choose to, after reviewing your own diff."
expect "Your real source remains under your control. Reviewer acceptance and portable-build coursework remain separate from this evidence checkpoint."
more "For rollback, use your own reviewed checkpoint to restore only this lesson's changes. Never reset unrelated work or delete progress snapshots. Back up course/university/progress together with the matching mission definitions; personal progress is intentionally Git-ignored."
end
finish
